\section{Introduction}

When a Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct agent attempts to boil water in ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, it must track temperature thresholds, container states, and heat source interactions across dozens of steps. Internal LLM reasoning hallucinates these state transitions---predicting water has boiled when the stove was never activated---triggering agent death with a $-100$ penalty. Deterministic external computation (environment simulators, code interpreters) eliminates such hallucinations by executing exact state transitions. The natural question: when should an agent route computation externally versus reasoning internally?

Existing routing approaches \cite{toolformer-2024,art-2023,chameleon-2024} frame tool selection as a capability or efficiency decision---matching subtasks to tools that can solve them. This framing ignores a potentially dominant signal: routing to \emph{avoid catastrophic failure}. Understanding what routing actually learns requires counterfactual analysis, but the standard per-step oracle approach degenerates under sparse rewards. On ScienceWorld, where non-zero reward arrives only at episode termination, 97.4\% of steps produce tied intermediate scores under both strategies across 245 episodes---no step-level signal exists to train a router (\S\ref{sec:collection}).

We propose \emph{episode-level counterfactual analysis}: run complete episodes under pure-internal (ReAct~\cite{react-2023}) and pure-deterministic strategies, then compare episode outcomes to generate routing labels (\S\ref{sec:collection}). Across 11 ScienceWorld task types (245 episodes), the full deterministic advantage is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$ points. Excluding death episodes, this gap collapses to $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$---a Death Avoidance Factor (DAF; \S\ref{sec:daf}) of 90.41\%. The per-task correlation between death rate differential and performance gap is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$), confirming death avoidance as the primary mechanism (\S\ref{sec:death-decomp}). Death rates differ sharply: 42.9\% for internal reasoning versus 16.3\% for deterministic computation. Of 245 episodes, 86 prefer deterministic, 9 prefer internal, and 150 show no preference---96.3\% of oracle decisions match always-deterministic (\S\ref{sec:baselines}).

Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Episode-level counterfactual methodology.} We introduce episode-level counterfactual analysis with death-aware labeling and the DAF decomposition metric, overcoming per-step oracle degeneracy in sparse-reward environments (\S\ref{sec:collection}, \S\ref{sec:daf}).
\item \textbf{Death avoidance dominates routing signal.} Across 11 task types (245 episodes), death avoidance accounts for 90.41\% of the routing signal ($\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$, 9.6\% of $\Delta_{\text{full}}$). Router training as a diagnostic probe confirms learnable routing structure at the representation level (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Method}

\subsection{Counterfactual Episode Collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Per-step oracle labeling assigns routing labels by comparing single-step outcomes under each modality. Under sparse terminal rewards, this degenerates: on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, 97.4\% of steps produce identical intermediate scores regardless of modality, yielding no routing signal. We instead perform episode-level counterfactual analysis.

For each task instance, we execute two complete episodes under identical initial conditions. The \emph{pure-internal} episode follows the ReAct paradigm \cite{react-2023}, where the LLM interleaves chain-of-thought reasoning with environment actions. The \emph{pure-deterministic} episode uses the environment simulator to compute exact state transitions. In both cases the LLM selects which action to take; deterministic computation replaces only the state-transition prediction---equivalent to giving the agent a perfect world model.

Each episode pair produces a tuple $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$, where $s$ denotes the terminal score and $d \in \{0,1\}$ indicates agent death. Episodes are capped at 30 steps. The LLM backbone is Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct served via vLLM. ScienceWorld spans 30 task types with a $-100$ death penalty; after prescreening, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 per type).

\subsection{Death-Aware Labeling}
\label{sec:labeling}

Each counterfactual pair receives an episode-level routing label based on $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$. Scores are integer-valued, so we use exact (in)equality. The label is assigned by four mutually exclusive cases:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Death asymmetry}: $d^{\text{int}} \neq d^{\text{det}}$ $\rightarrow$ surviving strategy preferred.
    \item \textbf{Both dead}: $d^{\text{int}} = d^{\text{det}} = 1$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
    \item \textbf{Score gap}: both survive, $s^{\text{det}} \neq s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ higher-scoring strategy preferred.
    \item \textbf{Tied alive}: both survive, $s^{\text{det}} = s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Death Avoidance Factor}
\label{sec:daf}

To quantify the contribution of death avoidance to routing signal, we define the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF):
\begin{equation}
    \text{DAF} = 1 - \frac{\Delta_{\text{no\_death}}}{\Delta_{\text{full}}}
\end{equation}
where $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is the mean score gap ($s^{\text{det}} - s^{\text{int}}$) across all episodes and $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}}$ is the same gap restricted to episodes where neither strategy results in death. DAF $= 1$ indicates all signal stems from death avoidance; DAF $= 0$ indicates none does. We report results in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:exp-setup}

We evaluate on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based interactive environment for elementary science tasks with sparse terminal rewards and a $-100$ death penalty. Using the methodology in \S\ref{sec:collection}, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 per type). The LLM backbone is Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct served via vLLM with episodes capped at 30 steps.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task death rate differential (internal minus deterministic) vs.\ $\Delta_{\text{full}}$. Each point represents one of 11 task types. Regression: $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$).}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

The full performance gap between deterministic and internal strategies across 245 episodes is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$ points. Excluding all episodes where either strategy results in death, the gap collapses to $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$. The resulting DAF (\S\ref{sec:daf}) is 90.41\%: approximately 90\% of the deterministic strategy's advantage is attributable to death avoidance.

This concentration is consistent across task types. The Pearson correlation between per-task death rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$; Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}). The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes versus 16.3\% for deterministic---a 2.6$\times$ ratio driving the bulk of the score difference.

The label distribution reflects this asymmetry: 86 \texttt{det\_preferred}, 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, 150 \texttt{no\_preference}. When internal reasoning does not cause death, it performs comparably to deterministic computation.

One task reveals residual non-death signal: \texttt{find-non-living-thing} ($n=40$) has zero deaths yet $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$, entirely from non-death differences. Among the 10 death-prone task types, however, death avoidance overwhelms non-death signal.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-46.40$, $-32.27$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Det. & $-9.47$ & [$-14.71$, $-4.29$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-28.67$, $-15.75$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-11.54$, $-1.70$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router & $-10.70$ & [$-16.33$, $-5.27$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 task types, 245 episodes). 95\% Bootstrap CIs. The oracle selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode; 96.3\% of its decisions match always-deterministic.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} reports episode-weighted mean scores and death rates. Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points, consistent with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$. The oracle improves only modestly to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of its decisions matching always-deterministic---\emph{oracle degeneracy} indicating the optimal routing policy closely resembles unconditional deterministic computation. Random routing ($-22.38$) falls between the two pure strategies.

Keyword-based routing fails structurally: ScienceWorld actions use generic verbs (\texttt{pick up}, \texttt{move}, \texttt{connect}) rather than science-specific terms, causing match rates to cluster at extremes---near-total (85.8\%, degenerating to always-deterministic) or negligible ($<$5\%, degenerating to always-internal).

The trained router (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) scores $-10.70$, 1.23 points below always-deterministic, with comparable death rate (17.6\% vs.\ 16.3\%).

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We investigate whether episode-level counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic probe rather than a routing system proposal.

\paragraph{Classification head.} A linear head appended to the LLM's hidden representations degenerates to always-deterministic (0\% internal recall) across all configurations (class weighting, focal loss, undersampling, temperature scaling).

\paragraph{Implicit router.} We fine-tune Qwen3-1.7B to internalize routing decisions. Training configuration significantly affects results. With gradient accumulation GA=8 (effective batch size 32), the model achieves generation accuracy of 65.4\% with internal recall of 51.5\%---the only non-degenerate configuration. With GA=1 (effective batch size 4), logit-level accuracy reaches 74.5\% (balanced accuracy 79\%), but generation degenerates to 53.3\% accuracy with only 26.7\% internal recall---a 21.2 percentage point logit--generation gap. Effective batch size determines whether learned patterns transfer to generation.

Across four seeds (GA=8), balanced accuracy is $58.1 \pm 0.8\%$---8 points above chance. Internal recall varies from 51.5\% to 81.8\%, indicating seed-dependent boundaries with consistent weak discriminability. End-to-end (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}): the router's 97.0\% simulator invocation ratio means it routes internally only 3\% of the time---weak signal with unstable thresholds, consistent with minimal routing structure beyond death avoidance.

\begin{abstract}
LLM agents in rule-governed interactive environments face catastrophic failures when internal reasoning hallucinates state transitions. Deterministic external computation (environment simulators, code interpreters) can prevent such failures, but what does routing between these modalities actually learn? Per-step oracle labeling degenerates under sparse rewards---on ScienceWorld, 97.4\% of steps produce identical scores regardless of modality. We propose episode-level counterfactual analysis: running complete episodes under pure-internal and pure-deterministic strategies to generate routing labels from episode outcomes. Across 11 ScienceWorld task types (245 episodes), we find that death avoidance accounts for 90.41\% of the routing signal between Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct internal reasoning and deterministic computation ($\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$, $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$; Pearson $r = 0.9698$, $p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$ across task types). When neither strategy causes agent death, the performance gap is small but non-negligible ($\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$, 9.6\% of the total gap). A trained router does not significantly outperform an unconditional always-deterministic strategy ($-10.70$ vs.\ $-9.47$), confirming that the value of deterministic computation in this environment is concentrated in preventing catastrophic failures, not in providing positive task guidance.
\end{abstract}

\section{Method}

\subsection{Counterfactual Episode Collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Per-step oracle labeling assigns routing labels by comparing single-step outcomes under each computation modality. Under sparse terminal rewards, this approach degenerates: on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, 97.4\% of steps produce identical intermediate scores regardless of modality, yielding no routing signal. We instead perform episode-level counterfactual analysis.

For each task instance, we execute two complete episodes under identical initial conditions (same random seed, same task variation). The \emph{pure-internal} episode follows the ReAct paradigm \cite{react-2023}, where the LLM interleaves chain-of-thought reasoning with environment actions. The \emph{pure-deterministic} episode uses the environment simulator to compute exact state transitions for each action. In both cases the LLM selects which action to take; deterministic computation replaces only the state-transition prediction, not action selection---equivalent to giving the agent access to a perfect world model.

Each episode pair produces a tuple $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$, where $s$ denotes the terminal score and $d \in \{0,1\}$ indicates agent death. We use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (vLLM) on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based environment spanning 30 task types with a $-100$ death penalty, with episodes capped at 30 steps. After prescreening, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 per type).

\subsection{Death-Aware Labeling}
\label{sec:labeling}

Each counterfactual pair receives an episode-level routing label based on $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$. ScienceWorld scores are integer-valued, so we use exact (in)equality with no threshold. The label is assigned by one of four mutually exclusive cases:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Death asymmetry}: $d^{\text{int}} \neq d^{\text{det}}$ $\rightarrow$ the surviving strategy is preferred.
    \item \textbf{Both dead}: $d^{\text{int}} = d^{\text{det}} = 1$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
    \item \textbf{Score comparison}: both survive and $s^{\text{det}} \neq s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ the higher-scoring strategy is preferred.
    \item \textbf{Tied alive}: both survive and $s^{\text{det}} = s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
\end{enumerate}

Label distributions are reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Factor}
\label{sec:daf}

To quantify the contribution of death avoidance to routing signal, we define the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF):
\begin{equation}
    \text{DAF} = 1 - \frac{\Delta_{\text{no\_death}}}{\Delta_{\text{full}}}
\end{equation}
where $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is the mean score gap ($s^{\text{det}} - s^{\text{int}}$) across all episodes and $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}}$ is the same gap restricted to episodes where neither strategy results in death. DAF $= 1$ indicates all routing signal stems from death avoidance; DAF $= 0$ indicates all signal stems from non-death performance differences. When $\Delta_{\text{full}} = 0$, DAF is undefined, as no routing signal exists to decompose; we report DAF only for task types where $|\Delta_{\text{full}}| > 0$.

Results using DAF are reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\subsection{Rule-Based Baseline}
\label{sec:rule}

We test keyword-based routing that selects deterministic computation when the action contains science-related verbs. Three keyword lists of decreasing breadth produce match rates of 85.8\% (v1, degenerating to always-deterministic), 4.9\% (v2), and 2.8\% (v3, both degenerating to always-internal). ScienceWorld actions use generic interaction verbs (\texttt{pick up}, \texttt{move}) rather than science-specific terms; no keyword granularity bridges the gap between near-total and negligible match rates.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task scatter plot: death rate differential (internal minus deterministic) vs.\ performance gap ($\Delta_{\text{full}}$). Pearson $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$). Each point is one of 11 ScienceWorld task types.}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

The full performance gap between deterministic and internal strategies across 245 episodes is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$ points. When we exclude all episodes where either strategy results in agent death, the gap collapses to $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$. The resulting Death Avoidance Factor (\S\ref{sec:daf}) is 90.41\%: approximately 90\% of the deterministic strategy's advantage over internal reasoning is attributable to death avoidance rather than superior task performance when both agents survive.

This concentration is consistent across task types. The Pearson correlation between per-task death rate differential (internal minus deterministic) and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$), as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}. Knowing how much more frequently the internal strategy dies is nearly sufficient to predict the full performance gap for a given task type. The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes compared to 16.3\% for deterministic computation---a 2.6$\times$ ratio that drives the bulk of the observed score difference.

The episode-level label distribution reflects this asymmetry: of 245 episodes, 86 are \texttt{det\_preferred}, only 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 \texttt{no\_preference}---when internal reasoning does not cause death, it performs comparably to deterministic computation.

One task reveals the residual non-death signal. \texttt{find-non-living-thing} ($n=40$) has zero deaths under both strategies yet shows $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$, entirely from non-death performance differences---deterministic computation can provide advantages through more accurate state tracking even when agents survive. However, among the 10 death-prone task types, death avoidance overwhelms non-death differences. When both modalities survive, the gap is small but non-negligible ($\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$, 9.6\% of $\Delta_{\text{full}}$).

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 90\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-44.89$, $-33.37$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-13.66$, $-5.11$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-27.75$, $-17.09$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-10.65$, $-2.43$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router & $-10.70$ & [$-15.25$, $-6.38$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 task types, 245 episodes). 90\% confidence intervals computed via bootstrap. The oracle selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode; 96.3\% of its decisions match the always-deterministic strategy.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} reports episode-weighted mean scores and death rates for four unconditional strategies. Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points, consistent with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$. The oracle, which selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode, improves only modestly to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of its routing decisions matching always-deterministic. This \emph{oracle degeneracy} indicates that the optimal episode-level routing policy closely resembles unconditional deterministic computation---the routing signal is insufficient to improve upon simply always choosing the deterministic strategy. Random routing ($-22.38$) falls between the two pure strategies, with a death rate (27.8\%) proportional to its 50/50 allocation.

Keyword-based routing (\S\ref{sec:rule}) fails structurally: match rates cluster at extremes (85.8\% or $<$5\%), precluding meaningful routing discrimination.

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

A natural question is whether the episode-level counterfactual labels can train a step-level router. We investigate this as a diagnostic probe of label informativeness rather than a routing system proposal, testing two architectures.

\paragraph{Classification head.} We append a linear classification head to the LLM's hidden representations, trained on episode-level routing labels. Across all configurations tested---class weighting, focal loss, minority undersampling, and temperature scaling---the classifier degenerates to predicting always-deterministic (0\% internal recall in all cases). The episode-level labeling granularity does not transfer to step-level classification through this architecture.

\paragraph{Implicit router.} We fine-tune a smaller model (Qwen3-1.7B) to internalize routing decisions within its generation process. Gradient accumulation is critical: with gradient accumulation of 1, the model fails to achieve meaningful routing discrimination (internal recall 26.7\%); with gradient accumulation of 8, the model becomes non-degenerate (internal recall above 50\% across all seeds). We report results for the non-degenerate configuration (gradient accumulation 8). At both the logit and generation levels, accuracy is 65.4\%, balanced accuracy 59.3\%, and internal recall 51.5\%---no logit-generation gap exists for this configuration, demonstrating a weak but above-chance routing signal beyond what a degenerate always-deterministic predictor captures.

\paragraph{Cross-seed stability.} Balanced accuracy is stable across seeds (57--59\%), but internal recall varies from 51.5\% to 81.8\%---the signal exists but is insufficient for practical routing, consistent with the death avoidance decomposition: when ${\sim}90\%$ of routing value is death avoidance, the learnable non-death signal is structurally thin.

\paragraph{End-to-end evaluation.} Deployed as an episode-level router (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}), the trained router scores $-10.70$ (90\% CI $[-15.25, -6.38]$) with 17.6\% death rate, allocating 97.0\% of episodes to deterministic computation. It did not significantly outperform always-deterministic ($-9.47$, $[-13.66, -5.11]$)---CIs overlap substantially. The few internally-routed episodes include cases where the agent dies, and misrouting cost exceeds the benefit of correctly routing rare internal-preferred episodes. This is a direct corollary of death avoidance dominance: any router that occasionally routes internally risks catastrophic failure, while always-deterministic avoids this risk entirely.

Three observations follow. First, logit-level success rules out uninformative labels, pointing to the episode-to-step granularity mismatch as the classification head bottleneck. Second, in death-dominated environments, always-deterministic is near-optimal and any learned router must overcome asymmetric misrouting cost (death vs.\ marginal score improvement). Third, when DAF $\approx 90\%$, router headroom is structurally limited to the ${\sim}10\%$ non-death signal.

\paragraph{Labeling quality.} We verify that the episode-level counterfactual labels are free of oracle circularity. Of 245 episodes, 0\% of oracle routing decisions are determined by tie-breaking rules---all are based on genuine score differences. The remaining 61.2\% of episodes are ties (46.9\% both-alive, 14.3\% both-died) and are excluded from training. Retraining the implicit router on strict-only (non-tie) labels yields balanced accuracy of 0.5729, consistent with the full-label result (0.5761 $\pm$ 0.005), confirming that the labeling methodology introduces no circular signal.

\section{Conclusion}

We presented an episode-level counterfactual analysis methodology for diagnosing routing behavior in modular LLM agent systems. Applied to 245 episodes across 11 ScienceWorld task types, our analysis reveals that death avoidance accounts for 90.41\% of the routing signal between internal LLM reasoning and deterministic computation ($\Delta_\text{full}$ = +29.60 vs.\ $\Delta_\text{nd}$ = +2.84). This concentration is corroborated by a near-perfect correlation between per-task death rate differentials and routing benefit ($r$ = 0.9698, $p$ = 8.12$\times$10$^{-7}$), the oracle router's 96.2\% agreement with always-deterministic, and a trained router's near-random balanced accuracy of 58.1\%.

Our study has several limitations. The analysis uses a single benchmark (ScienceWorld) with a single LLM backbone (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), and the $-100$ death penalty may amplify the measured DAF. Future work should extend to step-level counterfactual labeling for finer-grained diagnostics, evaluate generalization to environments with different failure modes (e.g., MATH, ALFWorld), and investigate whether stronger backbones exhibit richer non-death routing signals.

